              "text": "While anyone can move through the water in whatever way they find comfortable, there are **8 primary, well-defined swimming styles (strokes)** that are widely taught and recognized. \n\nThese eight styles are typically divided into two categories: **competitive (Olympic) strokes** and **utility/survival strokes**. Additionally, there are a handful of beginner and niche styles you can learn.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The 4 Competitive Strokes (The \"Big Four\")\nThese are the most common strokes. They are used in competitive swim meets (including the Olympics) and are the staples of lap swimming for fitness.\n\n*   **Freestyle (Front Crawl):** The fastest and most efficient of all swimming styles. It is performed face-down, using an alternating overhand arm motion and a continuous flutter kick.\n*   **Backstroke:** Very similar to the front crawl but performed lying on your back. Because your mouth and nose remain out of the water, it is easy to breathe, though you cannot see where you are going.\n*   **Breaststroke:** The oldest and slowest of the competitive strokes. It requires simultaneous, sweeping arm movements and a synchronized \"frog-like\" kick. It is highly technical but great for casual swimming as it allows you to keep your head above water if needed.\n*   **Butterfly:** The most physically demanding and visually dramatic stroke. It involves a symmetrical, overhand arm recovery paired with a continuous \"dolphin kick\" (where both legs move together in an undulating wave).\n\n---\n\n### 2. The 4 Utility and Survival Strokes\nThese strokes are generally not used in modern speed competitions, but they are incredibly useful for lifesaving, long-distance military operations, or resting in the water.\n\n*   **Elementary Backstroke:** This is swam on your back using a breaststroke kick and symmetrical arm movements. Instructors often teach the movement sequence as *\"Soldier, Monkey, Airplane.\"* It is highly efficient, uses very little energy, and is excellent for resting or survival swimming.\n*   **Sidestroke:** Swum on your side, using a scissor kick and a \"push and pull\" arm motion. Because it only requires one arm to propel you (while the other can remain still or hold something), it is the primary stroke used by lifeguards to tow a victim to safety.\n*   **Combat Sidestroke (CSS):** A highly specialized variation of the sidestroke developed by the US Navy SEALs. It combines elements of freestyle, breaststroke, and sidestroke. It is designed to be extremely stealthy, energy-efficient, and low-profile, allowing swimmers to cover long distances with heavy gear.\n*   **Trudgen:** Named after English swimmer John Trudgen, this hybrid stroke combines a scissor kick (like the sidestroke) with an alternating overhand arm stroke (like the front crawl). It was historically used for long-distance open-water swimming.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Beginner & Niche Styles\nIf you want to go beyond the standard eight, there are several other informal or specialized styles you can learn:\n\n*   **Dog Paddle:** The most basic and natural swimming style. Characterized by paddling the hands and kicking the feet face-down under the water. It is often the first instinctual movement toddlers and adult beginners use when learning to stay afloat.\n*   **Head-High Crawl (Water Polo Stroke):** A variation of the front crawl where you keep your head entirely out of the water. Used by water polo players to keep their eyes on the ball, or by lifeguards to keep eyes on a victim. \n*   **Mermaid Swimming (Monofin Swimming):** A modern niche discipline where you wear a single large fin on both feet (a monofin) and swim using a full-body dolphin kick. \n\n### Which ones should you learn?\n*   **For safety and leisure:** Start with **Freestyle**, **Breaststroke**, and **Elementary Backstroke**. This gives you a fast stroke, a stamina-saving stroke, and a survival resting stroke.\n*   **For fitness and endurance:** Learn the \"Big Four\" (**Freestyle, Backstroke, Breaststroke, and Butterfly**). Alternating between them during a workout prevents repetitive strain injuries and works every major muscle group in your body.",
